<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miskawayh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">932–<a href="/years/1030">1030</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Ibn Miskawayh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miskawayh was a Persian philosopher and historian who served as secretary and librarian to Buyid rulers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajarib-al-umam">Experiences of the Nations</a> is a major universal history and an eyewitness source for Buyid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. His ethical treatise Refinement of Character drew on Greek philosophy and became a classic of Islamic ethics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miskawayh.jpg" alt="Miskawayh" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tehran,_National_Library_MS_5944_Al-Fawz_al-Asghar_1r-1v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miskawayh, whose full name was Abū ʿAlī Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb Miskawayh al-Rāzī, was born in Rey, then under Ziyarid control, and came from Parandak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He may have been a Zoroastrian convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, though it seems more likely that one of his ancestors converted. He was fluent enough in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> to translate some pre-Islamic texts in that language into Arabic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his early career Miskawayh lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, where he served as secretary to Muhallabi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the Buyid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>. After long service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> he moved to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a>, where he spent seven years working with the Buyid vizier Abu 'l-Fadl ibn al-'Amid. In 966 a group of ghazis marched on the Library of Rey, and Miskawayh managed to save it.</p><p>After Abu'l-Fadl's death in 970 he continued to serve his son Abu'l-Fath, and in 975 he left with him for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. He later worked as a secretary and librarian for a series of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>, among them '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. Some contemporary sources linked him with the Brethren of Purity, claiming that some of his writings were used in compiling their Encyclopedia. He died in 1030 at Ray, then under Kakuyid control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Miskawayh was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a> whose influence on Islamic philosophy lies chiefly in ethics. His Tahdhib al-Akhlaq, the Refinement of Character, was the first major Islamic work on philosophical ethics; written for students, it treats practical ethics, conduct and the refinement of character. He separated personal ethics from the public realm and contrasted the liberating nature of reason with the deception and temptation of nature.</p><p>His Tajārib al-Umam, the Experiences of Nations, is a universal history from the beginning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> that stops near the end of the reign of '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, and is one of the first eyewitness chronicles of contemporary events by a Muslim historian, since he had access to the inner workings of the court. In Al-Fawz al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, Al-Fawz al-Akbar, he gave a Neoplatonic account of the soul's evolution through a chain of emanations from God, which Muhammad Hamidullah compared to Darwinian thought. He also made an Arabic translation of the Persian Eternal Wisdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miskawayh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Iraqi countryside in particular had declined, and the Buyids' reliance on iqta grants, which assigned the tax revenue of land to soldiers in place of salaries, was blamed by contemporaries such as the historian <b>Miskawayh</b> for further damaging cultivation, since the holders had little interest in maintaining irrigation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ray, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.6,51.43&amp;year=995" aria-label="Ray on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5849 878 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miskawayh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6299 1148h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6299 1148h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.6,51.43&amp;year=995">What was here in 995 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
